The terminal ranking view mirrors the HTML one: options in best-first
order, a bar whose length grows as the mean rank improves, and the mean
itself printed alongside.  We map the mean to a bar percentage with the
same \enquote{$1$ is a full bar, $N$ is the shortest} inversion the web
page uses, so a lecturer reading the terminal and a respondent reading the
phone see the same shape.  We reuse the bar-chart layout maths --- fitting
labels and the detail column to the console width --- but format the detail
column as a mean rank rather than a count, which is why this is a separate
renderer rather than a call into [[_render_terminal_bar_chart]].

<<functions>>=
def _render_terminal_ranking_stats(
    question: dict, results: dict, *, console: Console
):
    options = [
        _single_line_text(str(option)) for option in question["options"]
    ]
    option_count = len(options)
    mean_ranks = results["mean_ranks"]
    rank_counts = results["rank_counts"]

    labels = []
    percentages = []
    details = []
    for index in results["order"]:
        labels.append(options[index])
        mean = mean_ranks[index]
        if mean is None:
            percentages.append(0.0)
            details.append("not ranked")
        else:
            percentages.append(
                (option_count - mean + 1) / option_count * 100
            )
            details.append(f"avg {mean:.1f} ({rank_counts[index]} ranked)")

    detail_width = max((len(text) for text in details), default=0)
    available = max(console.width - detail_width - 4, 2)
    max_label_width = max((len(label) for label in labels), default=1)
    label_width = min(
        max_label_width, max(1, available - TERMINAL_BAR_MIN_WIDTH)
    )
    bar_width = max(1, available - label_width)

    table = Table.grid(padding=(0, 2))
    table.add_column(width=label_width, justify="left", no_wrap=True)
    table.add_column(width=bar_width, no_wrap=True)
    table.add_column(width=detail_width, justify="right", no_wrap=True)

    for label, percentage, detail in zip(labels, percentages, details):
        if len(label) > label_width:
            if label_width > 3:
                label = label[: label_width - 3] + "..."
            else:
                label = label[:label_width]
        table.add_row(
            Text(label),
            Bar(
                TERMINAL_BAR_SIZE,
                0,
                percentage,
                width=bar_width,
                color="cyan",
                bgcolor="grey23",
            ),
            detail,
        )

    console.print(table)
@

A pin result is spatial, which a terminal can only approximate, and the
approximation depends on the pin target.  An image cannot be drawn in a
terminal, so image questions get a coarse character-cell density grid.
Text questions can do better: the terminal can show the \emph{content
itself} --- the very thing respondents pinned --- and use colour as
the heat channel.  Whether that colour lands on the glyphs or on the
cells behind them is the [[pin_heat]] choice threaded down from the
[[view]] and [[watch]] commands.  Both views end with the same table
of commented pins.

<<functions>>=
def _render_terminal_pin_stats(
    question: dict,
    results: dict,
    *,
    console: Console,
    pin_heat: str = DEFAULT_PIN_HEAT,
):
    pins = results["pins"]
    console.print(f"Pins: {results['pin_count']}")
    if not pins:
        console.print("No pins yet.")
        return

    if question["kind"] == "pintext":
        _render_terminal_pin_text_heatmap(
            question, results, console=console, pin_heat=pin_heat
        )
    else:
        _render_terminal_pin_grid(results, console=console)

    comments = [pin for pin in pins if pin.get("comment")]
    if comments:
        table = Table("x", "y", "Comment")
        for pin in comments:
            table.add_row(
                f"{pin['x']:.2f}", f"{pin['y']:.2f}", Text(pin["comment"])
            )
        console.print(table)
@

The image grid is $16 \times 8$: terminal cells are roughly twice as
tall as they are wide, so halving the row count keeps the rendered
block visually square and the densities undistorted.  Each cell maps
its count to a density ramp scaled against the busiest cell, with the
crucial exception that \emph{any} non-empty cell skips the blank
character --- at lecture scale most cells hold a single pin, and
rounding those to invisible would blank the whole map.

<<constants>>=
TERMINAL_HEATMAP_COLUMNS = 16
TERMINAL_HEATMAP_ROWS = 8
TERMINAL_HEATMAP_RAMP = " .:-=+*#%@"
@

<<functions>>=
def _render_terminal_pin_grid(results: dict, *, console: Console):
    grid = bin_pins(
        results["pins"],
        columns=TERMINAL_HEATMAP_COLUMNS,
        rows=TERMINAL_HEATMAP_ROWS,
    )
    peak = max(max(row) for row in grid) or 1
    ramp = TERMINAL_HEATMAP_RAMP
    for row in grid:
        line = "".join(
            ramp[1 + int(count / peak * (len(ramp) - 2))]
            if count else ramp[0]
            for count in row
        )
        console.print(f"|{line}|", highlight=False)
@

The text view recovers character coordinates from the normalised pins.
The web form normalises clicks against the rendered content box, and
that box is exactly as wide as the longest line and as tall as the
line count, so multiplying $x$ by the longest line's length and $y$ by
the number of lines maps a pin back onto an approximate character
cell.  The column is approximate --- respondents' font metrics differ
--- which is why the horizontal kernel spreads heat a couple of
characters wide, while the row kernel stays nearly a point: the line
index survives the round trip reliably, and \enquote{which line}
is usually the question being asked.

Each pinned cell is then styled on the shared blue--green--yellow--red
ramp at its normalised heat; cells below a small floor keep the plain
terminal style.  The heat lands on the cell \emph{background} by
default: glyphs keep a legible text colour rather than dimming into
the theme at the cold end of the ramp, and heat over the padding that
fills each line to the grid width stays visible, so a pin past the
end of a short line still shows up --- foreground colour on a space
is invisible.  Foreground mode remains available through
[[--pin-heat]] or the [[mentipy.pin_heat]] config key for terminals
and capture pipelines that render background colour poorly.  The
content is deliberately \emph{not} syntax-highlighted in either mode,
unlike on the HTML pages: colour \emph{is} the heat channel here, and
code colouring would fight the density signal on the very glyphs the
lecturer is trying to read.

<<constants>>=
TERMINAL_PIN_TEXT_KERNEL = (1.0, 2.0, 4.0, 2.0, 1.0)
TERMINAL_PIN_TEXT_ROW_KERNEL = (1.0, 4.0, 1.0)
TERMINAL_PIN_HEAT_FLOOR = 0.05
@

<<functions>>=
def _render_terminal_pin_text_heatmap(
    question: dict,
    results: dict,
    *,
    console: Console,
    pin_heat: str = DEFAULT_PIN_HEAT,
):
    content_lines = str(question["content"]).splitlines() or [""]
    rows = len(content_lines)
    columns = max(len(line) for line in content_lines) or 1
    grid = smooth_pin_grid(
        bin_pins(results["pins"], columns=columns, rows=rows),
        kernel=TERMINAL_PIN_TEXT_KERNEL,
        row_kernel=TERMINAL_PIN_TEXT_ROW_KERNEL,
    )
    peak = max(max(row) for row in grid) or 1.0

    for row, line in enumerate(content_lines):
        text = Text()
        for column, character in enumerate(line.ljust(columns)):
            intensity = grid[row][column] / peak
            if intensity < TERMINAL_PIN_HEAT_FLOOR:
                text.append(character)
            else:
                text.append(
                    character,
                    style=_pin_heat_style(intensity, pin_heat),
                )
        console.print(text)
@

Rich composes foreground and background from one style string, so the
two heat channels differ only in what the style helper returns: the
bare ramp colour, or the ramp colour after [[on]] with a contrasting
glyph colour in front.  The glyph colour cannot stay the terminal's
default in background mode --- a light theme's dark text vanishes on
the blue and red stops, a dark theme's light text on the yellow one
--- so the helper picks black or white per cell from the perceived
luminance of the heat colour (the ITU-R BT.601 weights, which rate
green far brighter than blue).  On this ramp the threshold puts black
text on the bright yellow mid-ramp and white text on the darker blue,
green, and red stops.

<<constants>>=
PIN_HEAT_CONTRAST_LUMINANCE = 150
@

<<functions>>=
def _pin_heat_style(intensity: float, pin_heat: str) -> str:
    color = pin_heat_color(intensity)
    if pin_heat == "foreground":
        return color
    red, green, blue = (
        int(color[position : position + 2], 16)
        for position in (1, 3, 5)
    )
    luminance = 0.299 * red + 0.587 * green + 0.114 * blue
    if luminance > PIN_HEAT_CONTRAST_LUMINANCE:
        return f"#000000 on {color}"
    return f"#ffffff on {color}"
@

