\subsection{Preparing a run, and the runners}
\label{sec:server-runners}

Two commands start a server: [[serve]], and [[present]]
(\cref{sec:present}), which also follows the lecture in the same
terminal.  The preparation is the same for both --- the respondent URL,
the regenerated QR codes, the start-up lines, the server's options, and
under [[--public]] the exposure that must stay open while the server
runs --- so it lives in one function, together with the server options
both pass on unchanged, the page language among them, and the commands differ only in
the \emph{runner} they hand it.  The runner receives the start-up lines
together with everything the server needs.  [[serve]]'s runner prints
the lines and blocks in [[serve_http]], exactly as [[serve]] always
did; [[present]]'s keeps the lines as a banner.  Collecting the lines
in a list rather than echoing them is what makes that possible.

The public branch calls the runner inside the exposure context, so the
tunnel or port mapping lives exactly as long as the runner does, and
closes on Ctrl-C as on any other exit.  The local branch passes no
admin token, as before.

<<functions>>=
def _run_server(
    store: Store,
    host: str,
    port: int,
    *,
    public: PublicMode | None,
    results: bool,
    lock: bool,
    locale: str | None,
    runner,
):
    options = {
        "public_results": results,
        "allow_images": get_setting(ALLOW_IMAGES_CONFIG),
        "heatmap_bins": get_setting(HEATMAP_BINS_CONFIG),
        "lock": lock,
        "locale": locale,
        "default_locale": get_setting(LOCALE_CONFIG, DEFAULT_LOCALE),
    }
    if public is None:
        lines = _local_start_lines(
            store, host, port, results=results, lock=lock
        )
        runner(lines, store, host, port, **options)
        return

    admin_token, require_token = _admin_token_policy(public)
    with _public_url_context(public, port) as public_url:
        lines = _public_start_lines(
            store,
            host,
            port,
            public_url,
            results=results,
            lock=lock,
            admin_token=admin_token,
        )
        runner(
            lines,
            store,
            host,
            port,
            admin_token=admin_token,
            require_token=require_token,
            **options,
        )


def _serve_runner(
    lines: list[str], store: Store, host: str, port: int, **options
):
    for line in lines:
        typer.echo(line)
    with _reporting_bind_errors(host, port):
        serve_http(store, host, port, **options)
@

A port that is taken, or an address the machine does not have, is the
most common way for a start to fail, and the lecturer needs the
address and the reason, not a traceback.  Both runners therefore turn
a bind error into one line and exit status 1.  Only errors that come
from the socket are turned: the bind errnos without a file name, and
the resolver's [[gaierror]] for a host name that does not resolve.  A
store that cannot be written also fails with an [[OSError]], possibly
with [[EACCES]], but it names its file, and its traceback stays, since
\enquote{cannot listen} would point the lecturer at the wrong cause.
For [[serve]] the guard also covers [[serve_forever]], which raises
none of these errors, since the server is already bound by then.

<<imports>>=
import errno
import socket
<<constants>>=
BIND_ERRNOS = frozenset({errno.EADDRINUSE, errno.EADDRNOTAVAIL, errno.EACCES})
<<functions>>=
@contextmanager
def _reporting_bind_errors(host: str, port: int):
    try:
        yield
    except OSError as error:
        if not _is_bind_error(error):
            raise
        typer.echo(
            f"Error: cannot listen on {host}:{port}:"
            f" {error.strerror or error}",
            err=True,
        )
        raise typer.Exit(code=1) from error


def _is_bind_error(error: OSError) -> bool:
    if isinstance(error, socket.gaierror):
        return True
    return error.filename is None and error.errno in BIND_ERRNOS
@

The local start-up lines keep two behaviours of the old inline code.
A pinned base URL is trusted as it is, and the warning to recompile
appears only when QR codes were actually rewritten.  A resolved LAN
address always carries the warning, since it is the address most
likely to have changed since the slides were compiled.

<<functions>>=
def _local_start_lines(
    store: Store, host: str, port: int, *, results: bool, lock: bool
) -> list[str]:
    configured_base = _configured_base(None)
    if configured_base not in (None, "", "auto"):
        base = configured_base
        count = regenerate_qrs(store, base)
        lines = [_address_line(f"Serving at {base}", count)]
    else:
        base = resolve_base_url(configured_base, port)
        count = regenerate_qrs(store, base)
        lines = [_address_line(f"Serving at {base}", count, always=True)]
    if not results:
        lines.append(_format_index_hint(base, host, port))
    if lock:
        lines.append(_format_lock_hint(base))
    return lines


def _address_line(address: str, count: int, *, always: bool = False) -> str:
    if count == 0 and not always:
        return address
    return (
        f"{address}\n"
        f"Regenerated {count} QR codes; recompile your slides so "
        "they embed the new images."
    )
@

\subsection{Presenting: serving and following in one terminal}
\label{sec:present}

In a lecture the lecturer used to run two commands, [[mentipy serve]]
in one terminal and a bare [[mentipy watch]] in another, to see the
answers arrive without refreshing the projected results page too early
(\cref{sec:watch-active}).  [[mentipy present]] does both.  It takes
all of [[serve]]'s options and [[watch]]'s display options
([[--interval]], [[--window]], [[--pin-heat]]), through the very same
option objects, so help and defaults cannot drift apart.  It leaves out
[[watch]]'s PREFIX and [[--server]], since it always follows its own
server.  The page language comes with [[serve]]'s options: [[--locale]]
changes the respondents' pages, while [[present]]'s own terminal
output, the banner, the warning line and the watch, stays English like
the rest of the command line.

<<cli setup>>=
@cli.command(context_settings={"allow_extra_args": True})
def present(
    ctx: typer.Context,
    host: Optional[str] = HOST_OPTION,
    port: Optional[int] = PORT_OPTION,
    store: Optional[str] = STORE_OPTION,
    public: bool = PUBLIC_OPTION,
    results: bool = RESULTS_OPTION,
    lock: bool = LOCK_OPTION,
    locale: Optional[str] = LOCALE_OPTION,
    interval: float = WATCH_INTERVAL_OPTION,
    pin_heat: Optional[str] = PIN_HEAT_OPTION,
    window: str = RESULTS_WINDOW_OPTION,
    open_browser: bool = OPEN_BROWSER_OPTION,
    open_with: Optional[str] = OPEN_WITH_OPTION,
):
    """Run the polling server and follow the lecture in this terminal.

    Starts the server as `mentipy serve` does, opens its admin index in
    a browser, then shows, like a bare `mentipy watch`, the answers to
    the question the lecturer has opened.  The start-up lines stay on
    screen above it; under `--public ssh` they include the admin link,
    so keep this terminal off the projector.
    """

    heat_mode = _pin_heat_mode(pin_heat)
    window = _results_window(window)
    chosen_host = host or get_setting(HOST_CONFIG, DEFAULT_HOST)
    chosen_port = _configured_port(port)
    active_store = _configured_store(store)
    public_mode = _parse_public_mode(ctx, public, command="present")
    open_command = (
        _chosen_open_command(open_with) if open_browser else None
    )
    startup_warnings = (
        ["--open-with is ignored with --no-open"]
        if open_with is not None and not open_browser
        else []
    )

    def runner(lines, store, host, port, **options):
        _present_runner(
            lines,
            store,
            host,
            port,
            interval=interval,
            window=window,
            pin_heat=heat_mode,
            open_command=open_command,
            startup_warnings=startup_warnings,
            **options,
        )

    try:
        _run_server(
            active_store,
            chosen_host,
            chosen_port,
            public=public_mode,
            results=results,
            lock=lock,
            locale=locale,
            runner=runner,
        )
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        return
@

[[present]]'s runner binds the server first, with [[create_server]],
which marks the session start and releases the port if that fails.  A
port in use or a read-only store therefore ends the command with
[[serve]]'s error before anything else happens, and the watch never
starts against a server that does not exist.  The server then runs in
a daemon thread, while the main thread follows the lecture: the
foreground is the place for the loop the lecturer interrupts with
Ctrl-C.  The [[finally]] stops and closes the server however the loop
ends; Ctrl-C then unwinds through [[_run_server]], whose exposure
context closes the tunnel or port mapping, and [[present]] returns
quietly.  The daemon flag is only a backstop for an exit that skips
the [[finally]].  The server logs no requests, so nothing writes over
the display.

The watch asks the server's own address: the loopback address of a
wildcard bind, or the bound address itself, in the shared form of
\cref{sec:watch-active}.  Under [[--public ssh]] the request still needs
no admin token, so loopback works there too.

<<imports>>=
import logging
import threading

from mentipy.server import create_server
<<functions>>=
def _present_runner(
    lines: list[str],
    store: Store,
    host: str,
    port: int,
    *,
    interval: float,
    window: str,
    pin_heat: str,
    open_command: str | None,
    startup_warnings: list[str],
    **options,
):
    with _reporting_bind_errors(host, port):
        server = create_server(store, host, port, **options)
    banner = _PresentBanner(lines)
    server_url = _server_url_for(host, server.server_address[1])
    with _logging_into(banner):
        thread = threading.Thread(target=server.serve_forever, daemon=True)
        thread.start()
        try:
            for warning in startup_warnings:
                LOGGER.warning("%s", warning)
            if open_command is not None:
                _open_admin_index(
                    open_command,
                    host,
                    server_url,
                    options.get("admin_token"),
                )
            typer.echo(str(banner))
            console = _result_console()
            _follow_active_question(
                store,
                server_url,
                _watcher_factory(
                    store,
                    window=window,
                    pin_heat=pin_heat,
                    console=console,
                    banner=banner,
                ),
                interval=interval,
                console=console,
                banner=banner,
                keep_going=True,
            )
        finally:
            server.shutdown()
            thread.join()
            server.server_close()
@

\paragraph{Opening the browser.}
Once the server listens, [[present]] opens its admin index in the
lecturer's browser, so the lecture starts with one command and the
results pages are a click away.  The order is the point: after the
bind, so a port in use never sends the browser to a server that is not
there, and before the watch, which then runs until Ctrl-C.  The URL is
the server's own index at the address the watch asks
(\cref{sec:watch-active}), not the public URL: it needs no round trip
through a tunnel, and it works before anyone outside can reach the
server.  For a wildcard or loopback bind that address is a loopback
one, and the index treats a request from loopback as the lecturer's.
A server bound to one other address is reached through that address,
and its index refuses a request from there with 403, so [[present]]
does not open it; a banner warning names the URL and says why.  Under
[[--public ssh]] a loopback request is no longer admin either, so the
URL carries the run's token, which makes any address admin; the server
answers it as it answers the admin link in the banner, by setting the
admin cookie and redirecting to the bare index, so the token leaves
the address bar at once.  The token does pass through the opener's
command line, where other users of the machine can read it, so on a
shared machine a lecture under [[--public ssh]] should use
[[--no-open]].

The command comes from [[--open-with]], else from
[[mentipy.open_command]], else from the platform's default
(\cref{sec:open-command} in the store); [[--no-open]] skips the step,
for a lecturer whose browser is already open on the index.  An empty
[[mentipy.open_command]], or [[none]], makes [[--no-open]] permanent on
a machine.  [[--open-with]] together with [[--no-open]] contradicts
itself; [[--no-open]] wins, and a banner warning says the command was
ignored.

The command is split as a shell would split it, so a browser with
options fits in one setting, and a [[{url}]] in it marks where the URL
goes, for the browsers that want it inside an option; without one, the
URL is the last argument.  On Windows the POSIX rules would read the
backslashes of a path as escapes and turn [[C:\Users\me]] into
[[C:Usersme]], so there the split keeps backslashes and quotes, and
one pair of quotes around a token is then removed: a path with spaces
is written in quotes, one without may stand bare.

The process is started and left alone: its input and output go to the
null device, so it cannot write over the display, and it is detached
from the terminal, so the terminal's Ctrl-C does not reach it.  On
POSIX systems it runs in a session of its own; on Windows, where there
are no sessions, it gets a process group of its own, which Ctrl-C
does not reach, and no console.  [[present]] never waits for it in its
own thread, so a browser that stays running cannot hold it up; a
daemon thread waits for it instead, so that an opener that exits at
once, as [[xdg-open]] does once it has handed the URL on, is reaped
rather than left as a zombie for the rest of the lecture.

A command that cannot be started, one that does not exist or does not
parse, is no reason to stop the lecture.  It is logged as a warning,
which puts it in the banner's warning line, and [[present]] goes on to
the watch; the lecturer opens the index by hand.

<<constants>>=
OPEN_BROWSER_OPTION = typer.Option(
    True,
    "--open/--no-open",
    help="Open the server's admin index in a browser once it listens.",
)
OPEN_WITH_OPTION = typer.Option(
    None,
    "--open-with",
    metavar="CMD",
    help=(
        "Command that opens the browser; {url} marks the URL, else it"
        " is appended. Defaults to config setting"
        f" {OPEN_COMMAND_CONFIG}, else xdg-open, open on macOS or"
        " explorer on Windows."
    ),
    show_default=False,
)
<<functions>>=
def _chosen_open_command(open_with: str | None) -> str | None:
    if open_with is not None:
        return open_with
    configured = get_setting(OPEN_COMMAND_CONFIG)
    if configured is None:
        return default_open_command()
    if configured.strip().lower() in OPEN_COMMAND_OFF:
        return None
    return configured


def _open_admin_index(
    command: str, host: str, server_url: str, admin_token: str | None
) -> None:
    url = _admin_index_url(server_url, admin_token)
    if admin_token is None and not _reached_through_loopback(host):
        LOGGER.warning(
            "not opening %s: the server listens on %s only, and its index"
            " is admin only for a request from loopback; bind 127.0.0.1"
            " or 0.0.0.0 to have it opened",
            url,
            host,
        )
        return
    _open_browser(command, url)


def _reached_through_loopback(host: str) -> bool:
    if host in LOOPBACK_FOR_WILDCARD:
        return True
    try:
        return ipaddress.ip_address(host).is_loopback
    except ValueError:
        return host == "localhost"


def _admin_index_url(server_url: str, admin_token: str | None) -> str:
    if admin_token is None:
        return f"{server_url}/"
    return f"{server_url}/?admin={admin_token}"


def _open_browser(command: str, url: str) -> None:
    try:
        process = subprocess.Popen(
            _browser_argv(command, url),
            stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            **_detached_from_the_terminal(),
        )
    except (OSError, ValueError) as error:
        LOGGER.warning(
            "could not open the browser with %s: %s",
            command or "an empty command",
            error,
        )
        return
    threading.Thread(target=process.wait, daemon=True).start()


def _detached_from_the_terminal() -> dict:
    if sys.platform == "win32":
        return {
            "creationflags": getattr(subprocess, "CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP", 0)
            | getattr(subprocess, "DETACHED_PROCESS", 0)
        }
    return {"start_new_session": True}


def _browser_argv(command: str, url: str) -> list[str]:
    if sys.platform == "win32":
        argv = [_unquoted(arg) for arg in shlex.split(command, posix=False)]
    else:
        argv = shlex.split(command)
    if not argv:
        raise ValueError("no command to run")
    if any("{url}" in arg for arg in argv):
        return [arg.replace("{url}", url) for arg in argv]
    return [*argv, url]


def _unquoted(arg: str) -> str:
    if len(arg) >= 2 and arg[0] == arg[-1] and arg[0] in "\"'":
        return arg[1:-1]
    return arg
<<constants>>=
OPEN_COMMAND_OFF = ("", "none")
<<imports>>=
import shlex
@

The watch clears the screen whenever it switches question, and on
every refresh of an aggregate question.  The start-up lines would
vanish with the first clear, and with them the warning to recompile,
the [[/active]] URL and, under [[--public ssh]], the admin link the
lecturer needs all lecture.  They are therefore kept as a banner that
is reprinted after every clear.  All clearing goes through one helper,
so no clear can forget the banner; [[watch]] passes an empty banner,
and its output stays exactly what it was.  An open question is the
exception: its answers are appended without a clear, so on a long
transcript the banner scrolls away until the next question.

The server writes to the terminal too.  When a phone drops its
connection mid-response, [[handle_error]] in the server logs a
warning, and an SSH tunnel logs what its process says; nothing
configures logging in the command line, so Python's last-resort
handler would print these lines straight over the display.  Silencing
them would hide real trouble, such as a failing tunnel.  Instead the
banner is also a logging handler: while [[present]] runs it takes the
records of every [[mentipy]] logger, counts them and keeps the last
one, and its text gains a status line, reprinted with the banner.  The
logger stops propagating meanwhile, so a handler the user configured
on the root logger cannot print them over the display either; both
changes are undone when [[present]] ends.

<<constants>>=
LOGGER = logging.getLogger(__name__)
MENTIPY_LOGGER = "mentipy"
<<functions>>=
class _PresentBanner(logging.Handler):
    """The start-up lines, and a status line for warnings logged since."""

    def __init__(self, lines: list[str]):
        super().__init__(logging.WARNING)
        self.text = "\n".join(lines)
        self.warnings = 0
        self.last = ""

    def emit(self, record: logging.LogRecord):
        self.warnings += 1
        self.last = " ".join(record.getMessage().split())

    def __str__(self) -> str:
        if not self.warnings:
            return self.text
        return (
            f"{self.text}\n"
            f"Warnings: {self.warnings}, the last: {self.last}"
        )


@contextmanager
def _logging_into(handler: logging.Handler):
    logger = logging.getLogger(MENTIPY_LOGGER)
    propagate = logger.propagate
    logger.addHandler(handler)
    logger.propagate = False
    try:
        yield
    finally:
        logger.removeHandler(handler)
        logger.propagate = propagate
@

<<functions>>=
def _clear(banner: str | _PresentBanner = ""):
    typer.echo(CLEAR_SCREEN, nl=False)
    if banner:
        typer.echo(f"{banner}\n")
@

