@

The separator set is the one option whose natural values are control
characters.  A newline is the separator a list poll wants most, and
typing a literal newline into a shell argument is awkward, so the flag
accepts the two-character escapes [[\n]], [[\t]] and [[\r]] and
decodes exactly those.  We do not reach for Python's [[unicode_escape]]
codec: it treats the input as Latin-1 bytes, so a Swedish letter in the
set would come out mangled.  Three explicit replacements are enough and
cannot surprise.  [[None]] (flag absent) passes through untouched,
since it means the default set rather than an empty one.

<<functions>>=
def _decode_separator_escapes(separators: Optional[str]) -> Optional[str]:
    if separators is None:
        return None
    return (
        separators.replace("\\n", "\n")
        .replace("\\t", "\t")
        .replace("\\r", "\r")
    )
@

