@

Both display commands accept a pin-text heat channel.  The flag wins
over the config key so a lecturer can test the other channel without
editing persistent settings, and an unrecognised value is rejected
rather than silently mapped to a default --- the config file is edited
by hand, so a typo like [[backgrund]] must surface as an error, not as
a mysteriously wrong colour scheme.

<<functions>>=
def _pin_heat_mode(value: str | None) -> str:
    mode = value if value is not None else get_setting(PIN_HEAT_CONFIG)
    if mode not in PIN_HEAT_CHOICES:
        raise typer.BadParameter(
            f"pin heat must be one of {', '.join(PIN_HEAT_CHOICES)},"
            f" got {mode!r}"
        )
    return mode
@

Both display commands also take the window, which defaults to the
results page's: the answers since the server last started.  A reused
question shares its answer pool with every earlier lecture, and the
terminal tally the lecturer reads beside the projector should match the
projected one.  The store would quietly read an unknown window as
everything; a word typed on the command line is rejected instead, like
the pin heat.

<<functions>>=
def _results_window(value: str) -> str:
    if value not in RESULTS_WINDOWS:
        raise typer.BadParameter(
            f"window must be one of {', '.join(RESULTS_WINDOWS)},"
            f" got {value!r}",
            param_hint="--window",
        )
    return value
@

