Square brackets are everyday text in a programming course: an index
such as \texttt{xs[i]} in a prompt, a lookup as an option, a student's
comment on a pin.  Rich reads a bracketed word in a printed string as
style markup and swallows it, so \texttt{[i]} turns into italics and
disappears.  Rich also replaces emoji codes, and a slice is full of
them: the \texttt{:b:} in \texttt{s[a:b:c]} becomes a symbol.  Each
case puts such text where one renderer prints it: the heading every
kind shares, a bar label, a ranking row, a word-cloud row (split at
commas only, since the default splitter would break the word at its
brackets) and a pin comment.  The multiple-choice answer goes to the
first option, so the second option, the one with markup-like brackets,
is printed by the bar chart alone and not also by the mode line.

